00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:30,960
Dawson Dawson was one of the influencers that kind of changed the whole trajectory for Kali AI.

96
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:34,480
He was, he just happened to be like one of the first people we reached out to.

97
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:38,320
He responded to it and he wanted to work with us.

98
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:52,880
And it was like the really cool thing about Dawson and for any creators listening, like, I would really try to like emulate this because a lot of creators., if you ask them to do anything, they're like, no, I'm going to charge you for that.

99
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:54,240
No, that's outside of the scope.

100
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:55,440
No, I'm not doing that.

101
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,440
Dawson was the complete opposite.

102
00:07:57,920 --> 00:07:59,600
There was nothing he wasn't down to do.

103
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:04,320
He was always willing to take on more work and he wouldn't, he wasn't like demanding a rate for it.

104
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:06,800
So much so that I thought, Oh, Dawson, like, you know, thank you so much.

105
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:08,320
We're going to give you a bonus this month.

106
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:12,240
And like, he became like a friend and now he is like a very close friend.

107
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:25,520
And he, like, his willingness to just put out content, iterate on content, try different angles, different formats, it allowed us to quickly find what worked for Kali.

108
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:33,440
And once we found that format that worked, we could just like copy and paste it across the millions of fitness influencers out there.

109
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:44,240
And like, yeah, who knows if that would have been possible if not for Dawson's willingness to just work with us and like, uh, to give it a go rather than like nickel and diming us for every little request we made.

110
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:53,600
And yeah, it's like for creators out there, like I, a lot of times, like, I'll hear them complain like, oh, yeah, like, I don't like screw these brands.

111
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:55,120
Like, they don't treat us right.

112
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:56,880
So like, we're not going to treat them right.

113
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,640
But like, when Dawson did that, like, he was always.

114
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:01,760
so kind to us.

115
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:04,800
We were willing to do anything for Dawson, whatever he needed.

116
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:11,920
And like it's like when, but if you treat it, the relationship strictly as transactional, it's going to always be transactional.

117
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:13,680
And that's what it never was with Dawson.

118
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:15,040
So shout out, Dawson.

119
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:29,680
And such a great lesson for brands and apps too, like if you find that kind of creator that's willing to go the extra mile and feels more like a partner than a hired hand or whatever, it's like latch on to that and go the extra mile on your end.

120
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:35,680
So like when you see someone, you know, going the extra mile on their end reciprocate and it can build to something much better.

121
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:46,080
And if you're working with a bunch of creators who aren't that, you know, sometimes you do just, and I'm sure you all ended up working with so many creators, sometimes it just has to be more transactional.

122
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:53,360
But especially at the beginning, if you can find someone who feels a little bit more like part of the team, more invested, like that's just gold.

123
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:01,840
Yeah, especially in the early days when you're not working with that many creators, there's honestly like no excuse not to build these personal relationships with the creators.

124
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:16,240
You have the time, you have the bandwidth, like really foster these relationships because not only will that manifest itself in the content they produce, but also like these extracurricular activities, like you can have the creators go and refer their friends.

125
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:20,640
And like, if you're miserable to work with, they're not going to refer other creators to you.

126
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:25,760
But if they, if you're like the best brand they've ever worked with, they're going to be shouting it from their rooftops.

127
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:27,120
Hey guys, like, come over here.

128
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:28,320
Like, this is the place to be.

129
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:30,000
And that's what we always try to do.

130
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:37,840
Well, stepping back, what was the original thesis and plan for marketing that even led to first reaching out to Dawson?

131
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:41,360
And then if you would, kind of relate it to what your advice would be today.

132
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:46,000
And if you think things are different today or if you think people should kind of re run that same playbook.

133
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:48,640
Our like thesis stemmed from the fact that.

134
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:57,600
fact that we were a calorie tracker and we felt that the people that track their calories the most are people that are into health and fitness.

135
00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:02,640
And then when we looked in the health and fitness space, we saw that there are millions and millions of creators.

136
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:08,400
Probably there's probably more health and fitness creators than any other creator niche out there.

137
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:15,520
So there's all of these creators, but there's a finite number of brands available to these creators.

138
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:20,560
You know, you've got like protein powders, you've got athleisurewear, that's about it.

139
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:27,120
And they can only work with a certain number of creators.ors that never had a brand deal before.

140
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:32,640
And we finally gave them an opportunity to get a brand deal.

141
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:36,560
So that was like the first, like there was just a huge supply.

142
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:39,360
The, the, the well ran deep.

143
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:49,440
And then on top of that, the content that these creators were making made it like super easy to integrate Kali AI into those videos.

144
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:53,280
These creators were already making what I eat in a day videos.

145
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:57,600
In order to look like this, you have to eat like this.

146
00:11:58,560 --> 00:11:59,680
You like in those types of videos.

147
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:02,400
So it's like, and they were making like maybe one a week.

148
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:08,000
So it was super easy to ask them to just, oh, when you're showing your food in those videos, just scan it with Kali.

149
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:11,040
I and that was the thesis.

150
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:16,160
And then Dawson proved the thesis because like when he executed on the format, it converted very well.

151
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:17,520
So right, awesome.

152
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:22,000
There's a million other creators doing the same types of content, the same type of content as Dawson.

153
00:12:22,240 --> 00:12:23,600
So let's just copy and paste.

154
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:24,880
We did that.

155
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:27,280
And it worked tremendously well.

156
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:34,160
As for should this be the playbook for apps moving forward in today's environment?

157
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:41,040
I think like if all of those variables ring true for your app, then absolutely right now.

